{
 "id": "2025-zheng-eight",
 "name": "Eight Landscapes: Restoration and Preference Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the eight landscape photographs from Figure 1 of Zheng, Fang, Zhang, Liu & Huang (2025), \"The effects of landscape on visual preference and fatigue recovery among university students: Differences in gender, grade level and major\", PLOS ONE 20(8): e0330694 (CC BY 4.0): mountain, field, waterscape, lawn, desert, forest, artificial nature and flowering plants.\n\nParticipants view the eight photographs in random order, one minute each with a one-minute rest after each, as in the paper's recovery stage. They then rate every landscape (photo shown again) on a short Perceived Restorativeness Scale with one item per dimension (being away, fascination, coherence, compatibility; 1 = very unlikely, 7 = very likely) and on visual preference (0 = I don't like it at all, 4 = I like it a lot). Records are keyed by the paper's image id; the page source holds the paper's PRS means per landscape. Analysis as in the paper: one-way ANOVA across the eight landscapes with post-hoc tests, and comparisons by gender, year of study and major (landscape vs other).\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) no stress induction (TEM-8 English test), no control group and no blood pressure or heart rate; (2) online instead of a lab; (3) the paper does not list its four PRS items, so the wording comes from Hartig's PRS as reported in Frontiers in Public Health 10:801453 and from PRS-11 for coherence; (4) year of study offers all years, not only first and third year; (5) the 5-minute quiet sitting before the task is left out. The images are cropped slightly on the left to remove the figure panel letters.",
 "author": "Zheng, Fang, Zhang, Liu & Huang",
